The `[4 99]` span probe was already in the earlier Dart run. The 14-mutation corpus now runs in all three lanes.

## Probe cases now refused

All four are covered by the mutation corpus above:
- Duplicate child edges.
- Wrong token text.
- A collapsed synthetic NAME.
- A span ending inside `é`.

Non-DFS child ordering is refused too, through the "children out of preorder" mutation.

## Finding to review

The helper-token error coordinates differ by host, which I did not expect. I pinned them per host instead of asserting one value:

| Error | JVM | Node | Dart |
|---|---|---|---|
| INDENT error column | 2 | 2 | 0 |
| EOF error [line column] | [1 0] | [2 0] | [1 7] |

The byte spans agree on all three hosts. This matches §4.6's "not required to agree", but it means the Node and Dart helpers differ from the JVM's. You may want that recorded as known debt.

## Left open

